<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dunkirk</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dunkerque</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dunkirk is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> in the Nord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, close to the Belgian border. Part of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it changed hands repeatedly between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> bought it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II of England</a> in 1662. In 1940, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, more than 330,000 Allied soldiers were evacuated from its harbour and beaches.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dunkirk.jpg" alt="Dunkirk" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Velvet · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunkerque_hotel_de_ville_vu_port.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The northern, Dutch-speaking part of the old county became the core of the modern region of Flanders in Belgium, whose name came to cover all of Dutch-speaking Belgium, while French Flanders around Lille and <b>Dunkirk</b> remains part of France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…War the United Kingdom declared war on Germany on 3 September 1939, evacuated over 330,000 Allied troops from <b>Dunkirk</b> in 1940, held off the Luftwaffe in the Battle of Britain and, under Winston Churchill, fought on until joined by the Soviet Union and the United States in 1941.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Off the coast of Cornwall, Admiral Miguel de Horna's Armada of Flanders intercepted an Anglo-Dutch merchant convoy of 44 vessels escorted by six Dutch warships, sinking or capturing 20 ships, and returned safely to its base at <b>Dunkirk</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-18">18 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As the <b>Dunkirk</b> evacuation ended, Churchill told the Commons it was not a victory but promised that Britain would 'never surrender'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-4">4 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The surrender exposed the Allied flank as the <b>Dunkirk</b> evacuation gathered pace.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-28">28 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
